## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply a classic bit-twiddling pattern (popcount) to a 128-bit type by handling two 64-bit halves, staying branchless and efficient.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via masks and shifts
- Handling composite 128-bit types (hi/lo halves)
- Branchless aggregation of results

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef unsigned long long ull;
typedef struct { ull hi; ull lo; } uint128;

static inline unsigned long long popcount64(ull x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7F;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (unsigned int)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to decompose a 128-bit value, apply a 64-bit bit-reversal, and reassemble in swapped halves to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit-reversal via mask/shifts
- 128-bit composite type handling
- Endianness intuition for cross-half reversal

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a deterministic, branchless 128-bit bit-permutation using fixed masks, a common pattern in high-performance bit-twiddling tasks relevant to databases and graphics.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit word with two 64-bit halves
- Nibble-wise bit permutation via fixed masks
- Branchless, mask-and-shift composition

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 galacticShuffle128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M0 = 0x1111111111111111ULL;
  const uint64_t M1 = 0x2222222222222222ULL;
  const uint64_t M2 = 0x4444444444444444ULL;
  const uint64_t M3 = 0x8888888888888888ULL;

  uint64_t lo0 = x.lo & M0;
  uint64_t lo1 = x.lo & M1;
  uint64_t lo2 = x.lo & M2;
  uint64_t lo3 = x.lo & M3;

  uint64_t hi0 = x.hi & M0;
  uint64_t hi1 = x.hi & M1;
  uint64_t hi2 = x.hi & M2;
  uint64_t hi3 = x.hi & M3;

  uint128 r;
  r.lo = lo0 | (lo2 >> 1) | (lo1 << 1) | lo3;
  r.hi = hi0 | (hi2 >> 1) | (hi1 << 1) | hi3;
  return r;
}
```

